{
  "name": "Content/Governance Tiers",
  "slug": "content-governance-tiers",
  "domain": "components",
  "source": {
    "kind": "code-only",
    "why": "No Figma set. Its contract depends on required props and CSS state, neither of which a Figma variant can express.",
    "component": "src/components/GovernanceTiers.tsx",
    "styles": "src/components/GovernanceTiers.css"
  },
  "description": "How a component earned its way into the shared library: three tiers, three\ngates, and the route that was rejected drawn straight through all of them.\n\nIT ILLUSTRATES A REJECTED EXPLORATION, which decides its shape. The reader has\njust been told that letting any team push into core is the fastest way to\nfill a core library with components only one team can maintain. So the figure\nshows the ladder that shipped, and the control removes the gates rather than\ndescribing their absence: the rejected model IS this picture with three things\ntaken out of it, and that is a thing to show rather than a thing to say.\n\nNO COLOUR OF ITS OWN. Unlike the token-tiers figure beside it, this depicts a\nprocess rather than somebody else's palette, so there is nothing here that has\nto stay absolute. Every tone is a token, it repaints with the brand pack, and\nboth themes come out right without a second export or a second set of values.\n\nInteractive with no JavaScript, on the same mechanism: one native checkbox and\n`:has()`. A page that ships no runtime for a diagram can afford to have the\ndiagram do something.",
  "donts": [
    "Do not dim the gates to show them removed. Reducing opacity takes the one element whose whole job is to be read under AA; a strike says removed at full contrast.",
    "Do not use status/danger raw for the rejected route. It measures 2.94:1 on a light band and 5.23:1 on a dark one, so it fails AA in exactly one theme, which is the kind of bug that survives review. Mix it toward fg/primary first.",
    "Do not give the bypass a label on every panel. Three copies of the same two words is not emphasis, and the control above already names the route.",
    "Do not add a caption per panel where prose already sits beside the figure. An exploration states its reasoning in full next to this; a caption repeats the argument at a smaller size.",
    "Never let the toggle be anything but a real checkbox. The space bar, the focus ring and the announced state come free from the browser, and a div with a click handler has none of them."
  ],
  "props": {
    "alt": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The accessible name for the whole figure, and REQUIRED for the same reason `IconButton` requires `aria-label` and `TokenTiers` requires `alt`. A picture built from elements has no missing image for anyone to notice, so an undescribed one ships looking finished and nothing reports it."
    },
    "stages": {
      "type": "GovernanceStage[]",
      "required": true,
      "note": "Team, shared, core. Rendered in the order given, left to right."
    },
    "tiers": {
      "type": "GovernanceTiersLabels",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The three tier names, drawn on every panel."
    },
    "rejected": {
      "type": "GovernanceTiersRejected",
      "required": false,
      "note": "The rejected model, as a control rather than a caption. Optional: without it the figure is the shipped ladder and nothing else, which is the right thing to render anywhere the rejected path is not the subject."
    },
    "defaultRejected": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "required": false,
      "note": "Start with the rejected path showing. Off by default, because the shipped ladder is the honest resting state. It exists so a story can snapshot the second state, since Chromatic photographs a page rather than operating one — the same reason `TokenTiers` takes a `defaultBrand`."
    },
    "caption": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": false
    },
    "captionCopyRef": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": false,
      "note": "`<file>:<path>` into src/copy, making the caption editable in the browser under `npm run dev`. Dev tooling only; inert in a build."
    }
  },
